{
  "source": "Bies 5e Table 7.6, PDF pages 428-433 (printed pp. 399-404)",
  "about": "The ninety-four rows Table 7.6, \"Representative values of airborne sound transmission loss for some common structures and materials\", prints over six pages, sideways on the page, in nine bold groups: panels of sheet materials, panels of sandwich construction, single masonry walls, double masonry walls, stud partitions, single glazed windows, doubled glazed windows, doors and floors. Columns as printed: \"Panel construction\", \"Thickness (mm)\", \"Surface weight (kg/m²)\" and, under \"Octave band centre frequency (Hz)\", 63, 125, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000 and 8000. There is no single-number rating column: no STC, no Rw. The values are transmission loss in decibels, and the unit is stated in the caption rather than in the header. Thirty-eight rows leave the 63 Hz cell empty and thirty-six leave the 8000 Hz cell empty, most of them the same rows; the six bands between are filled on every row. Two rows print no surface weight, the proprietary acoustic door and the resiliently mounted gypsum ceiling. What the book says about the numbers is three lines, in section 7.2.8 on the page before the table (PDF page 427, printed p. 398): \"Results of transmission loss (field incidence) tests on conventional building materials and structures have been published both by manufacturers and testing laboratories. Some examples are listed in Table 7.6.\" That is all. No measurement standard is named, no mounting is described, no row is credited to a source, and there is no footnote under the table on any of its six pages. The one technical qualifier is \"field incidence\", and the caption's word is \"representative\". Two rows print \"joints scaled\" where \"sealed\" is meant, and another row of the same page prints \"sealed\" correctly; one group heading prints \"Doubled glazed windows\"; one floor row prints \"no of oak surface\". All are kept as printed. Three rows print a thickness that does not follow from the description, and one of them, the floating screed, describes 126 mm of concrete in a 190 mm assembly. The two plywood sets, one under \"Plywood sheets on wood framework\" and one under \"Plywood\", print the same thickness and surface weight with different values, which is the kind of disagreement a table compiled from several laboratories carries and does not explain.",
